'''2.''' The state of blossoming; the opening of flowers in general; flowers collectively: as, the plant is in ''bloom'', or covered with ''bloom''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ancient pear-trees that with spring-time burst
Into such breadth of ''bloom''.</poem>
{{right|''Bryant'', Among the Trees.}}}}

'''3.''' A state of health and growth promising higher perfection; a flourishing condition; a palmy time: as, the ''bloom'' of youth.
{{smaller block|<poem>He look’d, and saw a creature heavenly fair,
In ''bloom'' of youth, and of a charming air.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Wife of Bath, l. 531.}}}}
{{smaller block|In our sad world’s best ''bloom''. {{right|''Tennyson'', The Brook.}}}}

'''4.''' The rosy hue on the cheek indicative of youth and health; a glow; a flush.
{{smaller block|<poem>And such a lovely ''bloom'',
Disdaining all adulterated aids of art,
Kept a perpetual spring upon her face.</poem>
{{right|''Massinger'', Unnatural Combat, ii. 3.}}}}

'''5.''' A name sometimes given to minerals having a bright color: as, the rose-red cobalt ''bloom'', or erythrite, etc.—'''6.''' A powdery deposit or coating of various kinds. {{smaller|(''a'') The delicate, powdery, waxy coating upon certain fruits, as grapes, plums, etc., and leaves, as of the cabbage.}}
{{smaller block|The finest qualities of our nature, like the ''bloom'' on fruits, can be preserved only by the most delicate handling. {{right|''Thoreau'', Walden, p. 9.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The powdery appearance on coins, medals, and the like, when newly struck. (''c'') In ''painting'', a cloudy appearance on the surface of varnish. (''d'') The yellowish fawn-colored deposit from the tanning-liquor on the surface of leather, and penetrating it to a slight depth.}}
{{smaller block|In tanning it [rock chestnut-oak bark] is used unmixed, and gives a beautiful ''bloom''. {{right|''C. T. Davis'', Leather, p. 119.}}}}

