'''bloom<sup>1</sup>''' (blöm), ''v.'' [< ME. ''blomen'' (= MLG. ''blomen'' = Norw. ''bloma'', ''blōma''), bloom; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To produce or yield blossoms; flower, literally or figuratively.
{{smaller block|The first time a tree ''bloometh.'' {{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist.}}
<poem>The Lotos ''blooms'' below the barren peak.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Choric Song, viii.}}}}
'''2.''' To glow with a warm color.—'''3.''' To be in a state of healthful beauty and vigor; show the beauty of youth; flourish; glow.
{{smaller block|<poem>Hearts are warm’d and faces ''bloom.''</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, Epil.}}
<poem>A better country ''blooms'' to view,
Beneath a brighter sky.</poem>
{{right|''Logan'', A Tale.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To put forth, as blossoms.
{{smaller block|Behold, the rod of Aaron . . . ''bloomed'' blossoms, and yielded almonds.
{{right|Num. xvii. 8.}}}}
'''2.''' To impart a bloom to; invest with luster or beauty.
{{smaller block|Rites and customs, now superstitious, when . . . charitable affection ''bloomed'' them, no man could justly have condemned as evil.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Pol.}}}}

'''bloom<sup>2</sup>''' (blöm), ''n.'' [Not found in ME., but in late AS.; < AS. ''blōma'', a bloom of metal (glossed ''massa'' or ''metallum''; cf. ''blōma oththe dāh'', ‘bloom or dough’ (of metal); ''īsenes blōma'', a bloom of iron; ''gold-blōma'', lit. ‘gold-bloom,’ applied once (as elsewhere ''gold-hord'', ‘gold-hoard,’ ‘treasure’) figuratively to Christ as incarnated); not found in other languages in this sense,

